WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.280
So let's go back to
our usual roadmap.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.720
So today, we are
going to still focus

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:15.240
on closed-loop methods
for optimal control,

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.920
particularly through the
lens of dynamic programming

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.040
in a discrete time formulation.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.720
But we're going to extend the
formulation to the setting

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.300
whereby we have
uncertainty in our system.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:29.360
Particularly, we're going
to reason about disturbances

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.000
that might affect the
forward propagation

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.920
of the dynamics for the state.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:41.200
The setting that we're going
to discuss is a very famous one

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.940
and is referred to as the Markov
decision processes setting.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:54.040
So specifically, remember
the problem formulation

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:58.400
we've been discussing for
the past two, three lectures.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.260
So now we are in discrete time.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:03.660
So the update
equations for the state

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:13.540
are written as xk plus
1 equal f of k xk, uk.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.500
So this would have
been a setting

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.420
until the previous lecture.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.620
But now we are adding an
additional element, wk.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:27.360
wk is referred to
as a disturbance.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:37.020
And this is something that
affects the forward propagation

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.460
of our dynamics, and depending
on the specific application

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:42.520
may have different meanings.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:45.940
For example, if you want
to control an aircraft,

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:48.640
wk could be environmental
uncertainty,

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.700
for example, wind gusts.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:57.420
If we are trying to control the
financial market, like basically

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:01.810
we want to decide how
to buy and sell stocks,

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:06.250
wk may represent the underlying
uncertainty within the market

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:07.850
and so on and so forth.

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:11.490
So the idea is that you
observe the state x of k.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:16.450
You take a control uk.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:23.970
And then nature is going
to pinpoint a value of wk.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.430
This order is
somewhat arbitrary,

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:28.210
but you have to fix
an order in order

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:31.530
to make this model actionable.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:35.310
Now, as it was the case
of deterministic setting,

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:38.050
we are still assuming
that the controls need

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:44.410
to belong to some set capital
Q that is our set of controls

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:50.810
is in general constrained
within some set capital U.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:55.090
Now the disturbance w
are quantities that are

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:59.990
computed by nature in a way.

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:05.890
And so they are modeled
as random variables.

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:16.510
And we do place a key
assumption on the distribution

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:18.710
of such random variables w.

00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:21.990
Specifically, we assume
that the random variables

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:27.030
wk are distributed according
to some general probability

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:28.630
distribution.

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:29.930
It could be Gaussian.

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:33.310
It could be anything.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:37.390
That can be conditional on
the current value of the state

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:40.530
and the current
selection of the control.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:45.330
So you are in a state x of k.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.150
You get to see that state.

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:49.270
You pick a control u.

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:52.750
Based on that, there
is some realization

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:54.930
of the disturbance value.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.630
Of course, in some
setting, actually,

00:03:56.630 --> 00:03:59.850
this probability
distribution does not

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:01.470
need to depend on x and u.

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:03.130
That's perfectly valid.

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:05.890
What is important, though,
is that this probability

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:08.690
distribution is not
allowed to depend

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:11.570
on previous values
of the states.

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:17.130
So it's not allowed to depend on
xk minus 1, [COUGHS] xk minus 2,

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:17.630
and so on.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:21.730
So it's not allowed to depend
on the history of the process.

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:24.870
That's a fundamental assumption
we are making in our model.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.010
It's typically a
good assumption,

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:30.650
meaning that the realization
of your disturbances

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:33.358
and essentially uncertainty
in your environment

00:04:33.358 --> 00:04:35.650
only depends on the current
state and current selection

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:36.770
of the control.

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:38.730
But, for example,
if you're driving,

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:40.370
what the other
pedestrians might do

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:42.992
next could be modeled
as a disturbance.

00:04:42.992 --> 00:04:44.450
And, of course,
what the pedestrian

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:47.850
is going to do next depend
on where we are with our car

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:50.390
and what we plan to do
turning right or left.

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:53.790
So sort of makes sense.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:55.830
But there are cases
where actually you

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:58.670
would like to model
the dependency

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:00.630
on the previous
values of the states.

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:03.630
Well, that's not allowed
in this problem setting.

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:06.230
So it's a restriction, but
it's a mild restriction.

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:09.830
Again, why do we place these
restrictions in our settings

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:14.590
is to find a good compromise
between generality

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:17.990
of our problem formulation and
the problem formulation that

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:20.450
allow us to do some computation.

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:26.150
Basically, derive some recipes
to compute all our control

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:29.550
policies in an actionable way.

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:32.670
I'll come back to
this assumption later.

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:34.770
As it was the case of the
deterministic setting,

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.650
we are interested in
closed-loop control,

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:40.030
so we are interested in finding
controls that are expressed

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:42.890
as a closed-loop policy.

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:46.750
So a function pi that
maps any state in the work

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:51.270
into an optimal control action.

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:54.060
And finally, the last ingredient
in our problem formulation

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:55.660
is the cost.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:58.060
So the cost is going
to be expressed

00:05:58.060 --> 00:06:01.860
as the summation of a
terminal cost as usual,

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:05.140
plus a stage-wise cost as usual.

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:06.820
Now the stage-wise
cost could actually

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:09.900
depend on the disturbance.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:12.940
We'll see some examples later.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:16.740
And importantly, since
you have a dependency

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:19.260
of the cost on the
disturbance, that

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:22.420
cost itself is a
random variable.

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:25.380
So you want to translate that
random variable in something

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:28.820
deterministic that you can
minimize or maximize depending

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:32.340
on your setting in order to
define a notion of optimality

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:33.980
for your policy.

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:38.020
To do that, there are
different ways to do it.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:40.500
We, in this problem
formulation, select

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:44.880
to compute an expectation
over the cost.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:47.980
The expectation is with respect
to the realization of all

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:49.320
the disturbance variables.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:54.960
Make sense?

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:59.560
So main differences here are
that the state transition

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:02.000
function depends
on a disturbance w.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:07.680
W is a random quantity with
an arbitrary probability

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:10.260
distribution that is
constrained, though,

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:12.200
of being only conditioned
on the current value

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:13.300
of the state of control.

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:18.200
It cannot depend on previous
values of the state.

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:22.460
And the cost is allowed to
depend on the disturbance.

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:24.600
But since we have
such a dependency,

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.200
all this term is now
stochastic quantity.

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:29.160
So to reduce it to a
deterministic quantity,

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:30.420
we can optimize over.

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:32.060
We take an expectation of it.

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:34.440
And then the problem
is to find a policy

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:37.480
pi that optimizes such a cost.

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:38.460
So any questions here?

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:41.220
So this is important we
are all on the same page.

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:43.440
Otherwise, everything
I'm going to discuss next

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:45.140
is not going to make much sense.

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:45.920
Yeah.

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:49.160
Just to clarify you
said that at each stage,

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:51.700
the disturbance value is
dependent upon the disturbance

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:53.420
in the prior stage?

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:58.560
Unless only indirectly
through the state.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:01.100
So the state mediates your
dependency on the history.

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:02.820
Of course, the
evolution of the state

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:04.620
depends on the disturbance.

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:11.060
But everything that is related
to the history of the process,

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:14.460
previous states, previous
controls, previous disturbances,

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:18.100
all of that knowledge is
mediated through the realization

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:19.120
of the current state.

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:23.820
Yeah.

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:26.260
So does the cost
change depending

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:29.140
on when you sample the wk?

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:30.420
Yeah, absolutely.

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:34.179
Like for example, you are trying
to control an aircraft depending

00:08:34.179 --> 00:08:37.220
on what the actual realization
of your wind profile

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:40.360
is, the cost of the
trajectory is going to change.

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:42.860
So you're only trying
to do that, I guess,

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:44.660
during your iterations?

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:50.200
No, the problem is that you
want to find a policy that

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:55.160
is optimal with respect to
all possible realization

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:57.880
of those uncertainties.

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:01.240
And one way to collapse
all those realizations,

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:04.280
those uncertainties, in the
number you can optimize over

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:07.040
is to take the
expectation of your cost

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:09.880
with respect to all
possible realizations.

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:11.660
So maybe sometimes
you are unlucky.

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:12.700
Sometimes you are lucky.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:16.000
So you want to compute the
policy that on average is

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:17.140
going to do good enough.

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:19.560
I didn't know it was
always like the function.

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:22.800
Yeah, so it's the expectation
over all realizations

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:29.120
of the disturbances at time 0,
1, 2, all the way to N minus 1.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:30.260
Other questions here?

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:38.400
So this is the setting of
Markov decision processes.

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:40.700
Now, there are
variations, right?

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:44.000
So as we will discuss
later, maybe you're

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.850
not just interested in the
expected value of your cost.

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:51.030
Maybe you're also interested
in expectation plus some notion

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:52.370
of variability.

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:55.710
So you want to make sure that
on average, you do good enough.

00:09:55.710 --> 00:09:59.790
And there are no cases whereby
you could do terribly poorly.

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:03.830
Or another setting that we're
not going to cover in this class

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:05.790
is rather than modeling
the disturbance

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:08.470
as a stochastic
quantity, to model it

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:10.810
in an adversarial fashion.

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:14.230
So assuming that at
every point in time

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:18.390
the disturbance that is
realized is the worst possible.

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:21.990
That leads to minimax problems.

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:23.210
There is no stochasticity.

00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:27.230
Basically, it now becomes
a game against nature

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:29.910
with some sort of game
theoretical interpretation

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:31.910
whereby you try to
compute an action

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:34.470
and nature tries to compute
the other action that

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:36.530
is maximally damaging.

00:10:36.530 --> 00:10:39.950
Anyway, we're not going
to see those settings.

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:40.950
What about Markov?

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:43.030
Is that called--

00:10:43.030 --> 00:10:43.610
Markov.

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:44.970
So Markov mean--

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:47.410
Markov-- yeah, yeah.

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:50.210
I did not come up with this
formulation, unfortunately.

00:10:50.210 --> 00:10:53.970
Otherwise, I will receive
a couple Nobel Prizes.

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:56.570
I thought it was Mr. White
on the board by himself.

00:10:56.570 --> 00:11:00.250
Oh, and I guess it relied on it.

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:03.730
But I'm just curious why people
would expect you to falsify

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:06.010
vis-a-vis the entire.

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:09.170
I'd assume it would
really be like that.

00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:12.590
So, because remember that
when we compute the cost,

00:11:12.590 --> 00:11:14.150
the cost is additive.

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:19.690
It takes into account the cost
of the full state trajectory.

00:11:19.690 --> 00:11:21.410
But the cost of the
full state trajectory

00:11:21.410 --> 00:11:25.970
depends on the realization
of all the disturbances.

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:31.170
That said, the state
evolution is only

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:34.770
affected by the current
realization of the state.

00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:36.810
And as we will see,
dynamic programming

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:40.050
will try to break down
this complex cost that

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:44.310
depends on all these nested
realization of the disturbance

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:51.790
into expectation with respect
to one disturbance at a time.

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:54.870
Does this address your question?

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:56.290
Somewhat.

00:11:56.290 --> 00:11:56.790
Yeah.

00:11:56.790 --> 00:12:01.430
Does it rely-- or does the state
rely on a previous time step

00:12:01.430 --> 00:12:03.110
only with that one?

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:05.410
Well, as I was telling
your colleague,

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:11.510
keep in mind that once you move
into the new state, xk plus 1,

00:12:11.510 --> 00:12:28.070
xk plus 2 is only going to be
affected directly by wk plus 1.

00:12:28.070 --> 00:12:33.070
But xk plus 1 came from
here, and xk plus 1

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:35.630
was affected by wk.

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:41.090
So you can see how xk plus 2 has
a direct dependency on wk plus 1

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:44.730
and indirect dependency on wk.

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:47.730
So actually, each
state does indeed

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:51.410
depend on all previous
disturbance realizations,

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:54.670
but directly only on
the most current one.

00:12:54.670 --> 00:12:56.170
At least in this
formulation, that's

00:12:56.170 --> 00:12:57.390
how we set up the problem.

00:12:57.390 --> 00:12:59.450
Does it make sense?

00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:02.710
So xk plus 1, too,
doesn't depend on wk.

00:13:02.710 --> 00:13:04.130
Absolutely.

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:08.250
But that dependency, as I
was telling to your colleague

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:11.530
before, is mediated
through the realization

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:13.110
of the state xk plus 1.

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:17.230
All right.

00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:18.830
So a few key points.

00:13:18.830 --> 00:13:22.630
So this is discrete-time model,
obvious, is a Markovian model.

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:25.090
Markovian model
means that we do not

00:13:25.090 --> 00:13:29.330
have an explicit dependency
on the history of the process.

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:33.010
Everything is mediated through
the current value of the state.

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:36.250
Practically, what that means
is that the probability

00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:40.760
distribution for the stochastic
disturbance can only depend

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:47.400
on xk and uk, cannot depend,
for example, on xk minus 1.

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:50.520
The objective is to find
a closed-loop policy.

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:53.600
In stochastic setting,
this is even more important

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:56.760
since stochasticity is
bringing us left and right.

00:13:56.760 --> 00:13:58.720
So you really want
to have a policy that

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:02.660
maps wherever you are in the
state space into a control--

00:14:02.660 --> 00:14:05.320
in an optimal control action.

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:06.940
The cost is additive.

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:07.500
That's usual.

00:14:07.500 --> 00:14:11.240
That's same as in the
deterministic setting.

00:14:11.240 --> 00:14:14.580
And we use a
risk-neutral formulation.

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:15.520
What does that mean?

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:20.960
That in order to compute a cost,
here we take an expectation.

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:25.540
We do not model
additional considerations,

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:28.520
such as I was saying
before, variability

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:29.760
with respect to the average.

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:33.060
In some settings, for
example, even as a human,

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:35.200
when you try to
optimize your life,

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:39.380
you try to optimize
for your average income

00:14:39.380 --> 00:14:41.340
if you made a bet,
but also you try

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:42.980
to protect yourself
from the worst case

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:44.700
or from excessive variability.

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:46.560
So this formulation
is not doing that.

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:51.017
It's only modeling what is
going to happen on average.

00:14:51.017 --> 00:14:53.100
There are more sophisticated
formulations referred

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:55.180
to as risk-sensitive
when you add

00:14:55.180 --> 00:14:58.140
additional terms such as,
for example, a variance term

00:14:58.140 --> 00:14:59.162
and so on.

00:14:59.162 --> 00:15:00.620
The problem with
those formulations

00:15:00.620 --> 00:15:05.780
is that they make computation
much more complicated.

00:15:05.780 --> 00:15:09.900
In their most naive--

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:13.140
in the most naive approaches,
standard dynamic programming

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:14.380
equations no longer hold.

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:16.940
The principle of
optimality no longer holds,

00:15:16.940 --> 00:15:20.660
so you need to do additional
analysis in order to come up

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:22.240
with the practical algorithms.

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:24.940
Actually, this is a topic I've
done quite a bit of research

00:15:24.940 --> 00:15:25.940
in the past.

00:15:25.940 --> 00:15:27.980
Anyways, for this
class, we're only

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:30.980
going to focus on the
risk-neutral formulation.

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:35.260
The reason why the expectation
is nice is intuitively,

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:38.540
the expectation is
itself a linear function,

00:15:38.540 --> 00:15:41.320
so it allows you to chop the
problem in multiple stages

00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:42.860
to be very practical.

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:44.960
But we'll see that in a second.

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:50.040
Now, as I've also
mentioned some time ago,

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:52.740
different communities
use different notations.

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:57.360
So uk is a typical
notation in controls.

00:15:57.360 --> 00:16:01.560
Computer science typically
use the notation ak. a stands

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:02.200
for action.

00:16:02.200 --> 00:16:06.800
xk is the typical notation
for the state in control.

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:09.520
Computer science
typically uses s of k.

00:16:12.460 --> 00:16:15.160
So the transition
equations are typically

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:18.680
written according to a
transition function that

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:21.800
says what the value
of the next state is,

00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:24.960
depending on xk and ak.

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:30.760
So this is analogous
to what we've got here.

00:16:30.760 --> 00:16:32.720
And there are
additional terminologies

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:34.520
from other communities,
like, for example,

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:36.980
from operations research
and so on and so forth.

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:41.860
Fortunately, there is a paper
which is called "AI, OR--

00:16:41.860 --> 00:16:44.860
OR stands for operational
research-- and Control Theory:

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:47.560
A Rosetta Stone for
Stochastic Optimization."

00:16:47.560 --> 00:16:50.300
So if you get tripped
by the notation

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:53.140
among different communities,
this is a good paper.

00:16:53.140 --> 00:16:56.520
Your only job is to say, that's
the symbol we use in control.

00:16:56.520 --> 00:16:57.680
It seems crazy, right?

00:16:57.680 --> 00:17:00.780
But I mean, these days, probably
we're just going to ChatGPT.

00:17:00.780 --> 00:17:02.605
When I first had
this reference--

00:17:02.605 --> 00:17:03.480
was a few years ago--

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:04.480
ChatGPT was not there.

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:06.540
But anyway, the point
is different communities

00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:07.800
use different terminologies.

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:10.099
Don't get tricked.

00:17:10.099 --> 00:17:15.460
Now, the good news is that
because of the way we formulated

00:17:15.460 --> 00:17:19.859
the problem, specifically,
we use the expected value

00:17:19.859 --> 00:17:25.440
operator to map the random cost
into deterministic quantity

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:26.900
we can optimize over.

00:17:26.900 --> 00:17:29.740
And critically, we make
the Markovian assumption.

00:17:29.740 --> 00:17:31.500
Intuitively, these
two assumptions

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:35.410
allow us to chop the
problem into pieces.

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:38.970
Chopping means the
principle of optimality.

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:42.190
Specifically-- and the
intuition is the same.

00:17:42.190 --> 00:17:43.630
We're not going to derive it.

00:17:43.630 --> 00:17:46.570
But the proof is
not too complicated.

00:17:46.570 --> 00:17:52.050
Assume that you have an
optimal control policy pi star.

00:17:52.050 --> 00:17:55.290
That means that we have an
optimal policy at time 0,

00:17:55.290 --> 00:17:57.930
pi star 0, 1; optimal
policy at time 1,

00:17:57.930 --> 00:18:01.810
pi star 1; optimal
policy at time N minus 1,

00:18:01.810 --> 00:18:03.390
pi star N minus 1.

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:05.650
So an optimal policy
is indeed a sequence

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:09.610
of N policies, each one
mapping the current state

00:18:09.610 --> 00:18:12.370
into the current
optimal control.

00:18:12.370 --> 00:18:14.230
Consider the subproblem.

00:18:14.230 --> 00:18:17.570
We are at some state x of k.

00:18:17.570 --> 00:18:23.570
And we only care about
what happens after time k.

00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:28.610
Then the truncated policy is--

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:35.010
sorry, we start at time i. k
is still the random subscript.

00:18:35.010 --> 00:18:41.350
We start at time i
with the state x of i.

00:18:41.350 --> 00:18:46.710
And we look at detail
problems from time

00:18:46.710 --> 00:18:49.310
i all the way to capital N.

00:18:49.310 --> 00:18:53.230
Then the tail policy-- so the
policy, the sequence of policy

00:18:53.230 --> 00:18:57.750
from time i to time N minus 1
is optimal for the tail problem.

00:18:57.750 --> 00:19:00.350
Intuition the same as
deterministic setting.

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:02.830
The proof, a little
bit more involved.

00:19:02.830 --> 00:19:05.670
If you're interested
in the proof,

00:19:05.670 --> 00:19:09.870
a good reference is
represented by the work

00:19:09.870 --> 00:19:11.790
of Dimitri Bertsekas.

00:19:11.790 --> 00:19:14.710
Basically, his
optimal control books,

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:17.890
they're also referenced
in the syllabus.

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:22.250
He provided proof.

00:19:22.250 --> 00:19:25.870
Nothing particularly insightful,
so we're going to skip it.

00:19:25.870 --> 00:19:29.710
But, of course, as any
result, it has to be proven.

00:19:29.710 --> 00:19:33.130
Any questions here?

00:19:33.130 --> 00:19:36.152
So per se, the principle of
optimality is not so useful.

00:19:36.152 --> 00:19:37.610
It's something
interesting to know,

00:19:37.610 --> 00:19:41.290
but it doesn't really lend
to an actionable algorithm.

00:19:41.290 --> 00:19:44.370
My point here is to
show that to highlight,

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:46.930
this problem has a
specific structure

00:19:46.930 --> 00:19:51.450
that allows us to get to an
actionable algorithm, which

00:19:51.450 --> 00:19:53.970
is really dynamic
programming, but extended

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:56.330
to the stochastic setting.

00:19:56.330 --> 00:19:59.490
And as you will see in a
second, the dynamic programming

00:19:59.490 --> 00:20:01.370
algorithm in the
stochastic setting

00:20:01.370 --> 00:20:04.130
has exactly the same form
of dynamic programming

00:20:04.130 --> 00:20:05.810
in the deterministic setting.

00:20:05.810 --> 00:20:09.210
So we start from
the end, and then we

00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:14.610
start solving the problem going
backward one stage at a time.

00:20:14.610 --> 00:20:19.330
Easy thing to do is just to
show how the algorithm works.

00:20:19.330 --> 00:20:22.950
We start from the
last stage, capital N,

00:20:22.950 --> 00:20:27.530
and we set the
terminal cost JN x of n

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:31.550
as the terminal cost in
our cost formulation.

00:20:31.550 --> 00:20:35.870
And then we go one step
backward recursively where

00:20:35.870 --> 00:20:40.630
at each time stage k for
a given state x of k,

00:20:40.630 --> 00:20:44.110
we try to find the
optimal control

00:20:44.110 --> 00:20:51.350
uk that optimally trades
off the current cost

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:54.590
and the future cost-to-go.

00:20:54.590 --> 00:20:58.670
So same as deterministic setting
with the two differences.

00:20:58.670 --> 00:21:01.030
The transition function
now depends on wk.

00:21:01.030 --> 00:21:02.130
But that's obvious.

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:03.850
That's how we set
up the problem.

00:21:03.850 --> 00:21:06.870
Remember that this is xk plus 1.

00:21:06.870 --> 00:21:11.490
Jk plus 1, xk plus 1 is the
cost of the optimal problem,

00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:15.190
the tail problem, that
starts at time k plus 1

00:21:15.190 --> 00:21:16.690
at state xk plus 1.

00:21:19.470 --> 00:21:22.470
And then the key difference with
respect to deterministic setting

00:21:22.470 --> 00:21:24.710
is that this quantity
is stochastic.

00:21:24.710 --> 00:21:31.580
The current cost in
general depends on wk.

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:34.860
And the cost-to-go,
it's also stochastic

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:39.340
because the transition to
the next state is stochastic.

00:21:39.340 --> 00:21:43.020
So we have to take an
expectation with respect to wk.

00:21:43.020 --> 00:21:45.140
But importantly in
this computation,

00:21:45.140 --> 00:21:47.940
we only take an expectation
with wk as opposed

00:21:47.940 --> 00:21:52.140
to the full sequence of
disturbances from time 0 to time

00:21:52.140 --> 00:21:53.220
N minus 1.

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:57.580
So it's a dramatic
simplification.

00:21:57.580 --> 00:22:02.740
So you carry out this
computation for every stage k

00:22:02.740 --> 00:22:05.340
and every state x of k.

00:22:05.340 --> 00:22:11.700
And once you are done going
backward all the way to time 0,

00:22:11.700 --> 00:22:16.220
J star x0-- so the optimal cost
of the problem you're interested

00:22:16.220 --> 00:22:18.580
in starting from state x0--

00:22:18.580 --> 00:22:22.120
is equal to the end
solution of this recursion.

00:22:22.120 --> 00:22:24.020
And the optimal
policy is constructed

00:22:24.020 --> 00:22:30.040
by setting pi star x of k as
the minimizer of the Bellman

00:22:30.040 --> 00:22:32.040
recursion.

00:22:32.040 --> 00:22:34.220
So same as the
deterministic setting.

00:22:34.220 --> 00:22:35.720
Only difference is
that now you have

00:22:35.720 --> 00:22:39.480
to deal with the disturbances.

00:22:39.480 --> 00:22:42.280
So previously when we
made the assumption

00:22:42.280 --> 00:22:44.960
that the distribution
of disturbance to solve

00:22:44.960 --> 00:22:46.560
depend on the first effort.

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:50.520
Is the purpose of that to ensure
that the optimality is zero?

00:22:50.520 --> 00:22:51.760
That's right.

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:52.760
Correct.

00:22:52.760 --> 00:22:57.760
So in stochastic setting, in
order to keep the structure,

00:22:57.760 --> 00:23:00.320
you still need that additive
structure on the cost.

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:03.400
But then you also need
the Markovian assumption

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:06.280
on the distribution
of disturbances.

00:23:06.280 --> 00:23:10.880
That allows you to derive the
dynamic programming equations

00:23:10.880 --> 00:23:12.600
to solve the problem.

00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:16.160
And for the proof
of it, I refer you

00:23:16.160 --> 00:23:19.080
to the books of Dimitri
Bertsekas, who's

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:20.900
a celebrated professor at MIT.

00:23:20.900 --> 00:23:22.400
Actually, I was his
course assistant

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:27.300
when he was teaching
dynamic programming at MIT.

00:23:27.300 --> 00:23:29.980
Any questions?

00:23:29.980 --> 00:23:32.620
Let's do an example,
just to fix ideas

00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:34.720
about what's happening here.

00:23:40.860 --> 00:23:43.280
So let's consider the
following problem.

00:23:43.280 --> 00:23:45.580
So this is an inventory
control problem.

00:23:45.580 --> 00:23:49.700
So you have a warehouse, and
you are storing some stock.

00:23:49.700 --> 00:23:53.340
You're purchasing some new stock
and anticipating future demand.

00:23:53.340 --> 00:23:57.380
And, of course, once demand
for that stock is realized,

00:23:57.380 --> 00:24:00.180
then you ship it.

00:24:00.180 --> 00:24:03.480
So we define-- we now model
the problem as follows.

00:24:03.480 --> 00:24:09.160
So we let xk-- that's our
state, the stock available.

00:24:09.160 --> 00:24:13.140
For example, number of
refrigerators if our warehouse

00:24:13.140 --> 00:24:16.180
is storing refrigerators.

00:24:16.180 --> 00:24:20.300
Inventory uk is the
number of additional items

00:24:20.300 --> 00:24:24.800
that we get anticipating
future demand.

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:31.600
And wk is the demand that
gets realized at time k.

00:24:31.600 --> 00:24:36.080
The state at time k plus 1--
so the number of units of stock

00:24:36.080 --> 00:24:44.520
that are available at the next
stage is given by how much stock

00:24:44.520 --> 00:24:48.460
I have right now plus the
stock that we purchased--

00:24:48.460 --> 00:24:50.140
and we assume that
once we purchase it,

00:24:50.140 --> 00:24:53.160
it gets immediately delivered--

00:24:53.160 --> 00:24:56.440
minus the demand.

00:24:56.440 --> 00:24:59.100
So if we start with
two, we purchase one.

00:24:59.100 --> 00:25:00.200
We go to three.

00:25:00.200 --> 00:25:03.660
Then we have a demand of two,
so we ship two stocks out.

00:25:03.660 --> 00:25:07.720
I'm left with one
at the next stage.

00:25:07.720 --> 00:25:14.520
If the demand is larger than
the stocks that I do have,

00:25:14.520 --> 00:25:18.680
then the number of stocks at the
next time stage is equal to 0.

00:25:18.680 --> 00:25:23.390
And the demand that is
unfulfilled, it's unfulfilled.

00:25:23.390 --> 00:25:24.950
So that's why the
transition dynamics

00:25:24.950 --> 00:25:28.990
at the maximum between 0
and xk plus uk minus wk.

00:25:31.910 --> 00:25:33.990
Now our warehouse is very small.

00:25:33.990 --> 00:25:38.030
And so the maximum
amount of stock

00:25:38.030 --> 00:25:42.390
we can keep in our
warehouse is equal to 2,

00:25:42.390 --> 00:25:46.470
which means that xk plus uk
is less than or equal to 2.

00:25:46.470 --> 00:25:47.930
Of course, this
is a toy problem.

00:25:47.930 --> 00:25:49.710
The reason why I'm
making this assumption

00:25:49.710 --> 00:25:51.710
is since I'm going to do
the dynamic programming

00:25:51.710 --> 00:25:53.790
recursion by hand,
and I have to do it

00:25:53.790 --> 00:25:55.870
for each possible
value of the state,

00:25:55.870 --> 00:25:59.870
I don't want to do it for 25
different values of the state.

00:25:59.870 --> 00:26:06.830
I artificially somewhat
decrease the state space.

00:26:06.830 --> 00:26:09.610
Now, what about the disturbance?

00:26:09.610 --> 00:26:12.030
We need to specify a
probabilistic model

00:26:12.030 --> 00:26:15.150
for the disturbance to have a
complete problem formulation.

00:26:15.150 --> 00:26:18.150
So in this setting, I'm
assuming that the demand, which

00:26:18.150 --> 00:26:20.770
is our disturbance or
our stochastic element

00:26:20.770 --> 00:26:25.010
in the formulation, does not
depend on the control nor state.

00:26:25.010 --> 00:26:27.210
So is a simple setting.

00:26:27.210 --> 00:26:32.250
Specifically, we
assume that at time k,

00:26:32.250 --> 00:26:35.930
we have a 10% probability
that we have no demand,

00:26:35.930 --> 00:26:39.410
70% probability that we
have demand for one unit,

00:26:39.410 --> 00:26:44.610
and 20% probability that we
have demand for two units.

00:26:44.610 --> 00:26:46.170
Of course, the
prior distribution

00:26:46.170 --> 00:26:50.410
has to be normalized, so the
probability has to sum to 1.

00:26:50.410 --> 00:26:52.110
And finally, we have
to set up the cost.

00:26:52.110 --> 00:26:56.470
So the cost is defined as 0.

00:26:56.470 --> 00:26:57.890
So we assume first
of all that we

00:26:57.890 --> 00:27:01.530
are going to optimize operations
over a horizon of three steps

00:27:01.530 --> 00:27:03.170
into the future.

00:27:03.170 --> 00:27:05.690
And we assume that the
terminal cost is equal to 0.

00:27:05.690 --> 00:27:10.170
So no matter how much stock I'm
left with at the final time,

00:27:10.170 --> 00:27:10.950
I don't care.

00:27:10.950 --> 00:27:12.710
I just dispose of it at no cost.

00:27:12.710 --> 00:27:15.250
So the terminal cost is 0.

00:27:15.250 --> 00:27:18.610
And then the stage-wise
cost is as follows, is uk.

00:27:18.610 --> 00:27:23.510
So we assume that we spend a
$1 to buy one unit of stock

00:27:23.510 --> 00:27:26.990
plus a quadratic term.

00:27:26.990 --> 00:27:31.230
So this quadratic term has
the following interpretation.

00:27:31.230 --> 00:27:38.990
If after a purchase uk units
of stock and then the demand wk

00:27:38.990 --> 00:27:41.890
gets realized, if this
quantity is positive,

00:27:41.890 --> 00:27:45.870
meaning that I have a
positive amount of stock

00:27:45.870 --> 00:27:49.710
that I have to keep until
the next stage, that's

00:27:49.710 --> 00:27:51.910
an undesirable situation
because, of course, storing

00:27:51.910 --> 00:27:53.810
stock is expensive.

00:27:53.810 --> 00:27:58.510
And I penalize that with
the square of that value.

00:27:58.510 --> 00:28:01.190
So that's interpretation if
this quantity is positive.

00:28:01.190 --> 00:28:05.990
This quantity is negative
means that the demand is

00:28:05.990 --> 00:28:09.950
larger than the amount of
stock that I have available.

00:28:09.950 --> 00:28:14.590
I still penalize this
quantity with a square

00:28:14.590 --> 00:28:16.850
because, of course,
not fulfilling demand

00:28:16.850 --> 00:28:19.130
is also an
undesirable situation.

00:28:19.130 --> 00:28:22.870
For example, damage the
trust of my customers.

00:28:22.870 --> 00:28:24.510
Of course, this is
somewhat arbitrary,

00:28:24.510 --> 00:28:27.110
and in general, how you set
up the cost is arbitrary.

00:28:27.110 --> 00:28:29.850
You just try to
inject some notion

00:28:29.850 --> 00:28:35.010
of how happy you are with
respect to different conditions.

00:28:35.010 --> 00:28:40.890
Notice how all of this
depends on the realization

00:28:40.890 --> 00:28:45.490
of different disturbances
at time 0, 1, and 2.

00:28:45.490 --> 00:28:48.290
So transform all of these
into deterministic quantity

00:28:48.290 --> 00:28:53.690
by taking expectation
over w0, w1, and w2.

00:28:53.690 --> 00:28:58.170
So now the problem is you are
an operator for this warehouse.

00:28:58.170 --> 00:29:00.010
What is your optimal policy?

00:29:00.010 --> 00:29:05.350
So for all possible realization
of the state, say delta time 2,

00:29:05.350 --> 00:29:08.030
it just so happened
that x2 is equal to 1.

00:29:08.030 --> 00:29:10.450
What is the optimal thing
that you should be doing?

00:29:10.450 --> 00:29:11.550
You buy 1?

00:29:11.550 --> 00:29:12.610
You buy 0?

00:29:12.610 --> 00:29:14.980
What do you do?

00:29:14.980 --> 00:29:15.480
Yeah.

00:29:15.480 --> 00:29:19.440
It sounds like
you're requiring a--

00:29:19.440 --> 00:29:20.800
It depends on the type.

00:29:20.800 --> 00:29:23.880
So basically how much stock
you have run on your stock.

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:28.600
So if at time 1, you have one
unit of stock, what do you do?

00:29:28.600 --> 00:29:32.860
Do you purchase an additional
unit of stock, or you do not?

00:29:32.860 --> 00:29:34.600
If the demand is 2.

00:29:34.600 --> 00:29:38.040
Well, keep in mind
the sequence of events

00:29:38.040 --> 00:29:39.680
how we have specified it.

00:29:39.680 --> 00:29:41.580
So you get to serve the state.

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:45.680
So that time 1, you
have x1 unit of stock,

00:29:45.680 --> 00:29:48.760
but you don't know your
demand yet at time 1.

00:29:48.760 --> 00:29:53.820
Then you have to select how
much stock you want to purchase,

00:29:53.820 --> 00:29:56.360
and then the demands
get realized.

00:29:56.360 --> 00:29:59.040
So I'm guessing when you're
buying a stock, would you

00:29:59.040 --> 00:30:02.000
expect it to be 1, do
you just keep the 1--

00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:04.260
Well, why do you say it's
expected demand of 1?

00:30:04.260 --> 00:30:07.520
You expect the
demand of 1 with 70%.

00:30:07.520 --> 00:30:11.480
But it's also possible
that you get 0 with 10%.

00:30:11.480 --> 00:30:15.220
And it's also possible
that you get 2 with 20%.

00:30:15.220 --> 00:30:17.300
So it's not so clear-cut.

00:30:17.300 --> 00:30:21.140
So what you know is not what the
exact realization of disturbance

00:30:21.140 --> 00:30:21.680
is.

00:30:21.680 --> 00:30:25.020
It's probabilistically what the
realization of the disturbances

00:30:25.020 --> 00:30:25.660
might be.

00:30:25.660 --> 00:30:31.260
So that's the knowledge
you can leverage.

00:30:31.260 --> 00:30:32.520
I mean, we should not rely.

00:30:32.520 --> 00:30:34.340
So when you invest
in stocks, you

00:30:34.340 --> 00:30:37.780
don't know exactly what the
return on your Tesla stock

00:30:37.780 --> 00:30:38.980
is going to be.

00:30:38.980 --> 00:30:41.940
So at the end of the day, you
don't know what the return of--

00:30:41.940 --> 00:30:45.260
the value of your Tesla stock
is going to be at 12:00 PM.

00:30:45.260 --> 00:30:46.400
I try to anticipate that.

00:30:46.400 --> 00:30:48.180
So you have some
probabilistic model.

00:30:48.180 --> 00:30:52.160
Based on that, you want
to optimize operations.

00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:55.580
Yeah.

00:30:55.580 --> 00:30:58.660
Quickly, though, so you
said the constraints

00:30:58.660 --> 00:31:03.200
translates to not wanting
more than two units of it.

00:31:03.200 --> 00:31:07.340
But why does that translate
a constraint on the action

00:31:07.340 --> 00:31:10.220
as well?

00:31:10.220 --> 00:31:16.160
Well, here, basically, I'm
modeling that at the maximum,

00:31:16.160 --> 00:31:18.380
you can have two units of stock.

00:31:18.380 --> 00:31:20.840
So your warehouse has
some physical limit.

00:31:20.840 --> 00:31:25.960
It can't have more than N units.

00:31:25.960 --> 00:31:28.520
So how much you
store in a warehouse

00:31:28.520 --> 00:31:32.360
is how much you have right
now plus how much you purchase

00:31:32.360 --> 00:31:34.400
and gets delivered immediately.

00:31:34.400 --> 00:31:36.377
So that's the meaning
of this constraint.

00:31:36.377 --> 00:31:37.960
But there's also a
pedagogical meaning

00:31:37.960 --> 00:31:39.460
about why you use
these constraints.

00:31:39.460 --> 00:31:43.840
So this is a setting
whereby uk, your control,

00:31:43.840 --> 00:31:47.020
is constrained by a
function of the state.

00:31:47.020 --> 00:31:50.960
So this is a case whereby the
explicit dependency of the set

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:53.300
capital U on xk arises.

00:31:53.300 --> 00:31:54.160
OK.

00:31:54.160 --> 00:31:56.320
Now what was the
other thing you said?

00:31:56.320 --> 00:32:02.640
You said your stock xk plus
uk minus wk or only xk?

00:32:02.640 --> 00:32:05.100
Well, the reason is that--
so this is your warehouse.

00:32:08.200 --> 00:32:10.800
And you have this
amount of stock.

00:32:14.420 --> 00:32:17.500
I have only this space here.

00:32:17.500 --> 00:32:22.540
Then you purchase with
u this space here.

00:32:22.540 --> 00:32:25.160
And then the demand is
realized afterwards.

00:32:25.160 --> 00:32:26.840
So it's not
immediately realized.

00:32:26.840 --> 00:32:29.423
So basically, there is a moment
in time where you can't really

00:32:29.423 --> 00:32:30.640
fit more stuff in there.

00:32:30.640 --> 00:32:31.420
Yeah.

00:32:31.420 --> 00:32:33.140
So think about that
as something that

00:32:33.140 --> 00:32:36.860
happens after a
non-infinitesimal amount of time

00:32:36.860 --> 00:32:39.220
after you purchase your stock.

00:32:39.220 --> 00:32:42.980
Again, some of these assumptions
can be changed right here.

00:32:42.980 --> 00:32:45.420
I want to give you a realistic
example, but also something

00:32:45.420 --> 00:32:47.260
that is manageable to--

00:32:47.260 --> 00:32:49.900
and also highlights some of
the features of the problem

00:32:49.900 --> 00:32:51.060
formulation.

00:32:51.060 --> 00:32:53.660
So the question is, how
do you solve this problem?

00:32:53.660 --> 00:33:01.660
So the way you solve it is by
being disciplined and running

00:33:01.660 --> 00:33:03.160
a dynamic programming recursion.

00:33:03.160 --> 00:33:12.570
So specifically, you set the
terminal cost J3 x3 as a J3 x3.

00:33:12.570 --> 00:33:14.210
And for this problem
formulation we

00:33:14.210 --> 00:33:16.310
said that this terminal
cost is equal to 0.

00:33:19.010 --> 00:33:21.190
This is by how we
define the problem.

00:33:23.976 --> 00:33:30.710
And then you start going
one step backward in time.

00:33:30.710 --> 00:33:46.530
So now we have J2 x2 is
equal to the minimum over u2.

00:33:46.530 --> 00:33:49.530
It has to be greater than 0,
and it has to be less than

00:33:49.530 --> 00:33:51.390
or equal to x2 minus 2.

00:33:51.390 --> 00:33:53.130
So this is the
control constraint

00:33:53.130 --> 00:33:54.770
that we were discussing.

00:33:54.770 --> 00:34:13.610
Expectation over w2 of J
of x2, u2, w2 plus J3 x3.

00:34:19.610 --> 00:34:20.110
OK.

00:34:23.790 --> 00:34:27.889
Now from the recursion, we
know that this is equal to 0.

00:34:31.949 --> 00:34:38.010
So we've write down this
explicitly, or we get--

00:34:44.230 --> 00:34:54.030
what we get is J2 x2 equal
minimum of u2 between 0.

00:34:54.030 --> 00:34:59.110
I cannot buy negative amount
of stocks and x2 minus 2

00:34:59.110 --> 00:35:02.270
because of the capacity
constraints in our warehouse

00:35:02.270 --> 00:35:06.370
expectation with
respect to w2 of--

00:35:06.370 --> 00:35:11.810
now I'm going to write down
the stage-wise cost explicitly.

00:35:11.810 --> 00:35:13.430
So this is going
to be equal to--

00:35:18.290 --> 00:35:28.930
it's going to be equal
to u2 plus x2 plus u2

00:35:28.930 --> 00:35:34.450
minus w2 squared.

00:35:34.450 --> 00:35:36.490
There is no cost-to-go
because I already

00:35:36.490 --> 00:35:39.890
set that the cost-to-go
J3 is equal to 0 because

00:35:39.890 --> 00:35:41.190
of the previous recursion.

00:35:41.190 --> 00:35:44.910
Well, I use the
boundary condition.

00:35:52.570 --> 00:35:55.910
Any questions here?

00:35:55.910 --> 00:35:56.410
Yeah.

00:35:56.410 --> 00:35:59.650
2 minus x2, first one stands.

00:35:59.650 --> 00:36:01.010
2 minus x2.

00:36:01.010 --> 00:36:02.270
Yeah, 100%.

00:36:05.750 --> 00:36:06.810
Sorry about that.

00:36:13.590 --> 00:36:17.790
That's absolutely is correct
because the constraint is u2

00:36:17.790 --> 00:36:22.150
plus x2 less than or equal to.

00:36:22.150 --> 00:36:24.010
Yep, thank you
for noticing that.

00:36:27.270 --> 00:36:27.770
All right.

00:36:27.770 --> 00:36:30.430
So now that you
have written down

00:36:30.430 --> 00:36:37.670
the Bellman equation for
state x of 2 at stage 2, now

00:36:37.670 --> 00:36:42.510
you have to solve it for all
possible values of the states.

00:36:42.510 --> 00:36:45.670
What are the possible
values of the states?

00:36:45.670 --> 00:36:50.470
0, 1, and 2, because you can't
have more than two stocks.

00:36:50.470 --> 00:36:55.950
So which one-- OK, so
let's do it for J2 0.

00:36:55.950 --> 00:37:00.310
So, of course, in general,
a computer would do it.

00:37:00.310 --> 00:37:03.020
But it's useful for
you to carry out

00:37:03.020 --> 00:37:06.220
some of these Bellmanic
actions by hand.

00:37:06.220 --> 00:37:07.780
It's a bit annoying.

00:37:07.780 --> 00:37:11.060
There is no cleverness
or smartness, nothing.

00:37:11.060 --> 00:37:13.860
But it's helpful in order
to better appreciate

00:37:13.860 --> 00:37:15.180
what's happening here.

00:37:15.180 --> 00:37:16.940
Practically, in
your final exam, I

00:37:16.940 --> 00:37:19.400
will ask you to carry out
a Bellman equation by hand.

00:37:19.400 --> 00:37:22.800
So I like to say
it's like a vaccine.

00:37:22.800 --> 00:37:23.800
It's a bit annoying.

00:37:23.800 --> 00:37:26.920
But you do it once, and
then you are fine for life.

00:37:26.920 --> 00:37:28.540
So do it.

00:37:28.540 --> 00:37:30.060
Anyway, so let's do it for 1.

00:37:30.060 --> 00:37:33.900
Then maybe you can do it for
J2 1 and J2 2 by yourself.

00:37:33.900 --> 00:37:41.340
So for J2 0-- so now
we have minimum over u2

00:37:41.340 --> 00:37:43.500
greater than or equal to 0.

00:37:43.500 --> 00:37:46.300
Now we are solving
the tail problem

00:37:46.300 --> 00:37:51.060
where x2 is equal to 0, which
means that the constraint is now

00:37:51.060 --> 00:37:54.340
u2 less than or equal to 2
because we are plugging 0

00:37:54.340 --> 00:37:55.420
into x2.

00:37:55.420 --> 00:38:01.160
So u2 between 0 and 2 of
expectations with respect

00:38:01.160 --> 00:38:05.060
to w2 of this quantity here.

00:38:08.240 --> 00:38:11.120
So what's the expectation
of this quantity?

00:38:11.120 --> 00:38:17.740
Well, with a 10% probability--

00:38:21.640 --> 00:38:26.920
with 10% probability,
w2 is equal to 0.

00:38:26.920 --> 00:38:29.060
So with 10% probability--

00:38:32.280 --> 00:38:33.660
so this is a random variable.

00:38:33.660 --> 00:38:38.200
Its realization is for
a given selection of u2

00:38:38.200 --> 00:38:48.080
is u2 plus x2 is 0 plus
u2 minus 0 squared.

00:38:48.080 --> 00:38:50.540
I mean, I'm a bit
pedantic here, but--

00:38:55.000 --> 00:39:01.540
so that's the case
where w2 is equal to 0.

00:39:01.540 --> 00:39:08.580
Plus-- then I had the case where
w2 is equal to 1, which is 70%.

00:39:08.580 --> 00:39:11.420
So plus 0.7.

00:39:11.420 --> 00:39:16.420
I mean, as an aside, the
expectation of a random variable

00:39:16.420 --> 00:39:20.860
x is equal to the summation of--

00:39:20.860 --> 00:39:27.140
for i equal to 1 to N of what?

00:39:27.140 --> 00:39:30.420
So if you have random variables
that can take N values

00:39:30.420 --> 00:39:33.340
xi's, each 1 with
probability pi's.

00:39:33.340 --> 00:39:34.800
Here on this side.

00:39:34.800 --> 00:39:35.300
Yeah.

00:39:38.100 --> 00:39:39.440
So that's what I'm doing here.

00:39:39.440 --> 00:39:45.340
So it's 0.7 of u2--

00:39:45.340 --> 00:39:47.380
the state is still 0--

00:39:47.380 --> 00:39:50.760
plus u2 minus 1 squared.

00:39:54.220 --> 00:39:55.840
And then I have a plus.

00:39:59.040 --> 00:40:02.760
Should be 20%.

00:40:02.760 --> 00:40:19.640
So plus 0.2 of u2 plus 0
plus u2 minus 2 squared.

00:40:19.640 --> 00:40:24.280
And now I have to find the u2
that minimizes this expression.

00:40:24.280 --> 00:40:25.380
So does this make sense?

00:40:25.380 --> 00:40:27.800
Essentially, you have
unrolled the expectations.

00:40:27.800 --> 00:40:30.760
With respect to the three
possible realizations

00:40:30.760 --> 00:40:37.240
of the disturbance
w2, each one weighted

00:40:37.240 --> 00:40:42.060
with its respective
likelihood of happening.

00:40:42.060 --> 00:40:42.560
Yeah.

00:40:42.560 --> 00:40:47.960
And if we started with an
initial stock value of 1, then

00:40:47.960 --> 00:40:52.460
all of the first terms in those
parentheses should go 1, right?

00:40:52.460 --> 00:40:53.152
1, 1, 1.

00:40:53.152 --> 00:40:55.300
Yes.

00:40:55.300 --> 00:40:58.730
And here, you should also
change the constraint set,

00:40:58.730 --> 00:41:02.830
because now u2 can only
be between 0 and 1.

00:41:02.830 --> 00:41:04.470
So it's--

00:41:04.470 --> 00:41:05.570
You have 2 minus 1.

00:41:05.570 --> 00:41:08.688
So you have a change here, too.

00:41:08.688 --> 00:41:10.230
The probability
distribution wouldn't

00:41:10.230 --> 00:41:12.313
change because we're
assuming that the probability

00:41:12.313 --> 00:41:15.050
distribution is not conditional
on the exact value of the state.

00:41:15.050 --> 00:41:17.090
So these numbers
would be the same.

00:41:20.260 --> 00:41:24.860
OK, so this becomes a J2 0.

00:41:24.860 --> 00:41:30.430
The minimum over u2
between the 2 and 0

00:41:30.430 --> 00:41:36.390
of 0.1 u2, 0.7 u2, 0.2 u2.

00:41:36.390 --> 00:41:41.190
Of course, probability sum
to 1, so this is equal to u2.

00:41:41.190 --> 00:41:55.770
Plus u2 squared times
0.1 plus 0.7 u2 minus 1

00:41:55.770 --> 00:42:05.370
squared plus 0.2 u2
minus 2 squared, which

00:42:05.370 --> 00:42:09.930
is exactly what you have here.

00:42:09.930 --> 00:42:13.150
So you plug u2 equals
0, you get a value.

00:42:13.150 --> 00:42:15.110
You plug u2 equals
1, you get a value.

00:42:15.110 --> 00:42:17.510
You plug u2 equal
2, you get a value.

00:42:17.510 --> 00:42:20.690
You pick the u2 that gives
you the lowest value.

00:42:20.690 --> 00:42:28.370
It just so happens that
the optimal choice is 1.

00:42:28.370 --> 00:42:31.810
So that means that if you
happen at stage number 2

00:42:31.810 --> 00:42:34.890
to be in state x2 equals
0, then the optimum thing

00:42:34.890 --> 00:42:39.770
to do for you is to purchase 1.

00:42:39.770 --> 00:42:44.530
And Callum thinks-- that's also
what your colleague was hinting

00:42:44.530 --> 00:42:48.610
at, that since we have such a
high probability that we have

00:42:48.610 --> 00:42:53.990
a demand of 1, you want to buy
an additional stock so that you

00:42:53.990 --> 00:42:55.410
can fulfill it.

00:42:55.410 --> 00:42:58.630
So it's not too surprising.

00:42:58.630 --> 00:43:04.470
Anyway, so it turns out that
if you do this computation now

00:43:04.470 --> 00:43:10.350
for the state equal to
1, the state equal to 2,

00:43:10.350 --> 00:43:13.550
and then you go
back to J1 and 0,

00:43:13.550 --> 00:43:18.430
then the optimal value for
each possible initial condition

00:43:18.430 --> 00:43:21.350
is as follows.

00:43:21.350 --> 00:43:24.410
So we encourage you to do
some of these computations.

00:43:24.410 --> 00:43:26.190
Maybe not all, but at
least some of them.

00:43:26.190 --> 00:43:31.337
The result for each recursion
for each possible stage

00:43:31.337 --> 00:43:33.170
is in the spreadsheet
that I've linked here.

00:43:33.170 --> 00:43:35.830
So just click on the link,
and you get a spreadsheet

00:43:35.830 --> 00:43:38.430
with all the values so that
you can cross-check that what

00:43:38.430 --> 00:43:40.150
you're doing is correct.

00:43:40.150 --> 00:43:41.770
Yeah, keep in mind
this is a cost.

00:43:41.770 --> 00:43:43.890
So in general, the
lower the better.

00:43:43.890 --> 00:43:47.230
So yes, state 1 will be
the best to start with,

00:43:47.230 --> 00:43:53.230
which again makes
sense is in between.

00:43:53.230 --> 00:43:55.470
So you're not at risk
of missing demand.

00:43:55.470 --> 00:43:59.290
But at the same time, you don't
have to buy a lot of new stock.

00:43:59.290 --> 00:44:01.510
So roughly speaking,
it's not a surprise.

00:44:01.510 --> 00:44:04.810
And it's something in the
middle for this specific problem

00:44:04.810 --> 00:44:06.390
is a good place to be.

00:44:06.390 --> 00:44:08.570
So short answer is yes.

00:44:08.570 --> 00:44:12.530
So does this give us the optimal
policy to take from any state

00:44:12.530 --> 00:44:13.930
or in general?

00:44:13.930 --> 00:44:17.610
Or if we moved on to
concept 1 to 2 and then

00:44:17.610 --> 00:44:23.250
just we have some
w for our demand,

00:44:23.250 --> 00:44:24.850
will we have to read w or--

00:44:24.850 --> 00:44:25.650
No.

00:44:25.650 --> 00:44:26.850
Maybe not until--

00:44:26.850 --> 00:44:30.130
Optimal policy, because
of officially the demand.

00:44:30.130 --> 00:44:33.270
So if you look back,
all the data are fine.

00:44:33.270 --> 00:44:36.810
So no, the sequence of
policy, particularly 1 and 2,

00:44:36.810 --> 00:44:40.090
always going to give you
the best optimal thing to do

00:44:40.090 --> 00:44:41.710
at all stages, all states.

00:44:41.710 --> 00:44:44.610
You don't have to
optimize anything.

00:44:44.610 --> 00:44:46.410
So that's pretty powerful.

00:44:46.410 --> 00:44:47.075
Yeah.

00:44:47.075 --> 00:44:48.450
So that's a little
bit of problem

00:44:48.450 --> 00:44:49.492
with dynamic programming.

00:44:49.492 --> 00:44:51.880
As you'll see in
the homework, it

00:44:51.880 --> 00:44:54.340
does provide significant
computational savings,

00:44:54.340 --> 00:44:56.560
whereas with the
brute-force optimization.

00:44:56.560 --> 00:44:59.840
But still, it's
pretty time-consuming,

00:44:59.840 --> 00:45:02.520
particularly because
you have a dependency

00:45:02.520 --> 00:45:04.660
on the number of states.

00:45:04.660 --> 00:45:07.280
So in this case, the
state is one-dimensional.

00:45:07.280 --> 00:45:16.320
x can only-- it's just one
number with now x is a pair.

00:45:16.320 --> 00:45:18.760
Then you have to--

00:45:18.760 --> 00:45:21.448
your scaling goes quadratically
and cubically and basically

00:45:21.448 --> 00:45:23.240
exponentially with the
number of dimensions

00:45:23.240 --> 00:45:24.615
in terms of how
many computations

00:45:24.615 --> 00:45:25.960
you have to carry out.

00:45:25.960 --> 00:45:28.600
In this case, specifically is 9.

00:45:28.600 --> 00:45:30.340
It's basically 3 for the states.

00:45:30.340 --> 00:45:33.600
And then you have to
go-- you have 3 stages.

00:45:33.600 --> 00:45:34.420
So 3 by 3.

00:45:34.420 --> 00:45:34.920
Yeah.

00:45:34.920 --> 00:45:38.200
So if you take it down,
it is a finite set

00:45:38.200 --> 00:45:41.200
of data that can be
hardened from-- do they

00:45:41.200 --> 00:45:42.180
the same for that one?

00:45:42.180 --> 00:45:43.440
Yeah.

00:45:43.440 --> 00:45:45.260
So if you have a
continuous state space,

00:45:45.260 --> 00:45:48.240
you cannot run this algorithm
because you cannot enumerate all

00:45:48.240 --> 00:45:49.660
possible states.

00:45:49.660 --> 00:45:51.320
So you can do a few things.

00:45:51.320 --> 00:45:57.580
So one could be discretizing
your state, of course,

00:45:57.580 --> 00:45:59.720
or map your state
representation,

00:45:59.720 --> 00:46:02.200
something lower dimensional,
and then discretize it there.

00:46:02.200 --> 00:46:04.450
But yeah, at some point, you
have to get to something.

00:46:04.450 --> 00:46:09.220
You can do numerical
optimization now.

00:46:09.220 --> 00:46:10.840
Let's look at another example.

00:46:10.840 --> 00:46:13.840
So our preferred
problem, which is LQR.

00:46:13.840 --> 00:46:18.300
So we're going to look at
LQR with yet another twist.

00:46:18.300 --> 00:46:20.120
So same setting as before.

00:46:20.120 --> 00:46:22.740
We have a quadratic cost.

00:46:22.740 --> 00:46:24.980
Only difference is
that now our dynamics

00:46:24.980 --> 00:46:28.340
are affected by a
disturbance w, which

00:46:28.340 --> 00:46:31.460
is stochastic quantity
that is distributed

00:46:31.460 --> 00:46:35.980
as a Gaussian variable
with 0 mean and covariance

00:46:35.980 --> 00:46:37.440
matrix sigma.

00:46:40.620 --> 00:46:43.580
So LQR problem as before.

00:46:43.580 --> 00:46:46.980
Only difference is that the
dynamics are not stochastic.

00:46:46.980 --> 00:46:49.720
So the question is, can
we use dynamic programming

00:46:49.720 --> 00:46:51.040
to solve this problem?

00:46:51.040 --> 00:46:51.940
The answer is yes.

00:46:51.940 --> 00:46:54.520
In a way, it is extremely
similar to what we

00:46:54.520 --> 00:46:57.280
did a couple of lectures ago.

00:46:57.280 --> 00:47:04.040
Specifically, the way
it works is that--

00:47:04.040 --> 00:47:08.840
inspired by what we
know from the solution

00:47:08.840 --> 00:47:11.600
to LQR in a
deterministic setting,

00:47:11.600 --> 00:47:20.800
let's assume that
the cost Jk xk is

00:47:20.800 --> 00:47:33.000
equal to some quadratic
plus possibly some constant.

00:47:33.000 --> 00:47:36.320
Let's call it ck.

00:47:36.320 --> 00:47:43.920
Remember that for LQR, the--

00:47:51.308 --> 00:47:52.640
there is a typo here.

00:47:52.640 --> 00:47:55.540
This should be Pk plus 1.

00:47:55.540 --> 00:47:58.820
I'll fix that.

00:47:58.820 --> 00:48:00.900
So remember that
in standard LQR,

00:48:00.900 --> 00:48:02.480
the cost-to-go is quadratic.

00:48:02.480 --> 00:48:05.660
It's a quadratic
form on the state.

00:48:05.660 --> 00:48:09.275
OK.

00:48:09.275 --> 00:48:11.360
Oh, come on, another typo here.

00:48:11.360 --> 00:48:14.020
So this should be xk.

00:48:14.020 --> 00:48:15.720
I don't know what
I was thinking.

00:48:15.720 --> 00:48:17.980
So this should be Jk or xk.

00:48:17.980 --> 00:48:20.920
And this should be xk plus
1 Pk plus 1, xk plus 1.

00:48:20.920 --> 00:48:22.620
So I'll fix these later.

00:48:22.620 --> 00:48:24.160
So how do we solve this problem?

00:48:24.160 --> 00:48:26.880
We write down the
Bellman equation.

00:48:26.880 --> 00:48:30.380
So Jk xk-- again, k
plus 1 is a typo here--

00:48:30.380 --> 00:48:32.220
is equal to minimum
of uk expectation

00:48:32.220 --> 00:48:35.940
over wk, stage-wise
cost plus cost-to-go.

00:48:35.940 --> 00:48:39.540
So assuming the
cost-to-go is a quadratic,

00:48:39.540 --> 00:48:42.860
so is xk plus 1, Pk
plus 1, xk plus 1.

00:48:42.860 --> 00:48:43.800
Plus a constant.

00:48:43.800 --> 00:48:47.810
I'm ignoring it here.

00:48:47.810 --> 00:48:51.870
Now let's unroll this
multiplication here quite fast,

00:48:51.870 --> 00:48:54.890
because this is essentially
something we already covered.

00:48:54.890 --> 00:49:00.530
So we have A x, B
u, Pk A x plus B u.

00:49:00.530 --> 00:49:04.370
So that's exactly the quadratic
term we had in standard LQR.

00:49:04.370 --> 00:49:10.210
Plus, I had quadratic
term in wk, which is here.

00:49:10.210 --> 00:49:16.090
Plus, I have the cross terms
between A x and B u and w,

00:49:16.090 --> 00:49:17.150
which is here.

00:49:17.150 --> 00:49:18.770
And I have two of them.

00:49:18.770 --> 00:49:22.190
So this is just by unrolling
this multiplication.

00:49:25.130 --> 00:49:28.330
So now I'm taking the
expectation of this quantity.

00:49:28.330 --> 00:49:32.050
So this is a constant.

00:49:32.050 --> 00:49:34.930
So its expectation
is exactly x Q x.

00:49:34.930 --> 00:49:36.930
This is a constant,
so the expectation

00:49:36.930 --> 00:49:39.770
is the same quantity.

00:49:39.770 --> 00:49:40.810
This is just a constant.

00:49:40.810 --> 00:49:42.310
Does not depend on wk.

00:49:42.310 --> 00:49:44.870
The only two things
that depend on wk

00:49:44.870 --> 00:49:47.430
is this term and this term.

00:49:47.430 --> 00:49:50.150
This term is linear in wk.

00:49:50.150 --> 00:49:52.910
And you assume your
wk is zero mean.

00:49:52.910 --> 00:49:55.030
So the expectation
of all of this stuff

00:49:55.030 --> 00:49:58.310
is 0 because the
expectation of w is 0.

00:49:58.310 --> 00:50:00.310
So if you have a
random variable--

00:50:00.310 --> 00:50:05.270
say that you have A times x,
you know that expectation of x

00:50:05.270 --> 00:50:10.910
is equal to 0, what is the
expectation of A x for A

00:50:10.910 --> 00:50:12.310
being a real number?

00:50:17.190 --> 00:50:17.790
0.

00:50:17.790 --> 00:50:19.390
A times expectation of x.

00:50:19.390 --> 00:50:22.430
Expectation of x is 0,
so this is equal to 0.

00:50:22.430 --> 00:50:25.750
So that's exactly
what's happening here.

00:50:25.750 --> 00:50:26.510
Where is it?

00:50:26.510 --> 00:50:27.130
Here.

00:50:27.130 --> 00:50:28.630
OK.

00:50:28.630 --> 00:50:32.390
The term that is not obvious
is what is the expectation

00:50:32.390 --> 00:50:38.670
of w Pk plus 1 w?

00:50:38.670 --> 00:50:41.450
So it turns out we're
not going to prove it.

00:50:41.450 --> 00:50:48.450
Basically relies on some basic
facts related to linear algebra,

00:50:48.450 --> 00:50:51.710
is that the
expectation of w Pk w,

00:50:51.710 --> 00:50:56.810
where w is a Gaussian variable
with covariance sigma,

00:50:56.810 --> 00:51:03.430
is equal to the trace of Pk plus
1 times the covariance of w.

00:51:03.430 --> 00:51:06.770
Remember that the
trace is equal to sum

00:51:06.770 --> 00:51:10.090
of the elements of
the diagonal matrix.

00:51:10.090 --> 00:51:12.770
This is not obvious.

00:51:12.770 --> 00:51:16.990
I'm just giving this
to you as a fact.

00:51:16.990 --> 00:51:19.970
So basically, I'm telling
you that the expectation

00:51:19.970 --> 00:51:29.770
of w transpose Pk plus 1 wk
is equal to the trace, which

00:51:29.770 --> 00:51:34.370
is some of the elements of the
diagonal of the matrix of Pk

00:51:34.370 --> 00:51:39.490
plus 1 times the
covariance sigma

00:51:39.490 --> 00:51:42.910
or the variables wk that we
assume we know because we assume

00:51:42.910 --> 00:51:45.430
that we know that
disturbance is a zero mean

00:51:45.430 --> 00:51:47.690
and with a given
covariance sigma.

00:51:51.950 --> 00:51:54.990
The proof requires
two or three steps.

00:51:54.990 --> 00:51:57.630
Not particularly insightful,
so we're skipping it here.

00:51:57.630 --> 00:52:01.190
The point I want to
make here is that look

00:52:01.190 --> 00:52:05.310
at what we found, that the
right-hand side of the Bellman

00:52:05.310 --> 00:52:07.910
equation is exactly
the one that we had

00:52:07.910 --> 00:52:11.470
in LQR plus a constant term.

00:52:11.470 --> 00:52:13.150
So these are quantities
that we already

00:52:13.150 --> 00:52:16.590
know, Pk plus 1
from the computation

00:52:16.590 --> 00:52:19.070
that we already carried
out at time k plus 1.

00:52:19.070 --> 00:52:23.310
And sigma is part of our
modeling knowledge, which

00:52:23.310 --> 00:52:27.310
means that when we try to
find the optimal control

00:52:27.310 --> 00:52:31.390
u, what would be the solution?

00:52:31.390 --> 00:52:34.390
It's exactly equal
to deterministic LQR

00:52:34.390 --> 00:52:35.410
plus a constant.

00:52:35.410 --> 00:52:37.690
So when I try to find
the optimal control,

00:52:37.690 --> 00:52:41.000
I take the derivative
with respect to u.

00:52:41.000 --> 00:52:43.380
The constant goes away because
it doesn't depend on u.

00:52:43.380 --> 00:52:46.780
So the solution that I will
find is exactly the solution

00:52:46.780 --> 00:52:50.100
that we found for LQR,
linear feedback on the state.

00:52:50.100 --> 00:52:53.980
I plug that back into the cost.

00:52:53.980 --> 00:52:58.780
I get a quadratic with respect
to the state plus a constant.

00:52:58.780 --> 00:53:00.860
So we started from a
cost-to-go at time k plus 1.

00:53:00.860 --> 00:53:02.660
That was a quadratic
plus a constant.

00:53:02.660 --> 00:53:05.100
I get again a quadratic
plus a constant.

00:53:05.100 --> 00:53:11.900
So all of this to say without
spending too much time here

00:53:11.900 --> 00:53:16.660
is that for stochastic LQR,
the control policy is the same.

00:53:16.660 --> 00:53:18.900
The cost is almost the same.

00:53:18.900 --> 00:53:21.020
The only difference
is that the cost

00:53:21.020 --> 00:53:23.620
is increased by some
constant related

00:53:23.620 --> 00:53:29.020
to the magnitude of the
noise, which makes sense.

00:53:29.020 --> 00:53:32.220
We have to pay something
because now we are operating

00:53:32.220 --> 00:53:36.400
in an uncertain setting.

00:53:36.400 --> 00:53:39.800
Anyway, this was just one
other example to showcase

00:53:39.800 --> 00:53:45.540
the use of stochastic dynamic
programming for the LQR problem.

00:53:45.540 --> 00:53:46.040
Yeah.

00:53:46.040 --> 00:53:47.566
Now do you want
people to-- do you

00:53:47.566 --> 00:53:49.360
want me to say this is
a deterministic case

00:53:49.360 --> 00:53:53.378
if the covariance was
dependent on the control?

00:53:53.378 --> 00:53:54.920
This part really is
like a function--

00:53:54.920 --> 00:53:55.760
Yeah, yeah.

00:53:55.760 --> 00:53:59.080
Of course, because
now once you start

00:53:59.080 --> 00:54:02.000
taking the derivative
with respect to u,

00:54:02.000 --> 00:54:04.220
this term wouldn't
go to 0 anymore.

00:54:04.220 --> 00:54:07.000
So actually, the formulation--
yeah, absolutely.

00:54:09.600 --> 00:54:18.240
What we exploit here
is that sigma is just

00:54:18.240 --> 00:54:20.600
a given quantity that
does not depend neither

00:54:20.600 --> 00:54:21.780
on state nor control.

00:54:26.840 --> 00:54:27.440
Cool.

00:54:27.440 --> 00:54:28.320
All right.

00:54:28.320 --> 00:54:32.160
So one additional
item-- maybe we're

00:54:32.160 --> 00:54:36.420
going to conclude this
item on Wednesday.

00:54:36.420 --> 00:54:38.660
This will become
important when we

00:54:38.660 --> 00:54:41.620
move into the second part of
this class, namely reinforcement

00:54:41.620 --> 00:54:44.900
learning and imitation learning.

00:54:44.900 --> 00:54:48.140
So, so far, we have introduced
stochastic optimal control

00:54:48.140 --> 00:54:50.500
problems or optimal
control problems

00:54:50.500 --> 00:54:52.200
in a finite-horizon setting.

00:54:52.200 --> 00:54:54.780
So basically, we assume that
we are optimizing operations

00:54:54.780 --> 00:55:00.220
over 15 steps, 20
steps, 35 steps.

00:55:00.220 --> 00:55:04.460
But as we will see for a
number of reasons, the RL

00:55:04.460 --> 00:55:06.280
problem or the imitation
learning problem,

00:55:06.280 --> 00:55:08.660
particularly RL problem,
personal learning problem,

00:55:08.660 --> 00:55:12.122
is formulated within an
infinite-horizon setting.

00:55:12.122 --> 00:55:13.580
So we assume that
now we are trying

00:55:13.580 --> 00:55:18.960
to optimize operations over
an infinite number of stages.

00:55:18.960 --> 00:55:22.260
So the number of
stages is infinite.

00:55:22.260 --> 00:55:25.740
Now, of course, the assumption
of an infinite number of stages

00:55:25.740 --> 00:55:28.360
is never fulfilled in practice.

00:55:28.360 --> 00:55:30.720
Of course, our
lifetime is limited.

00:55:30.720 --> 00:55:36.700
The lifetime of the universe
is arguably limited,

00:55:36.700 --> 00:55:40.240
but is a good approximation
for those problems

00:55:40.240 --> 00:55:46.520
involving a finite but very
large number of stages.

00:55:46.520 --> 00:55:49.640
And as we will see, it
simplifies the problem

00:55:49.640 --> 00:55:50.300
considerably.

00:55:50.300 --> 00:55:52.040
So it's again a
sort of a compromise

00:55:52.040 --> 00:55:54.240
between being
something that is quite

00:55:54.240 --> 00:55:57.520
reasonable in many settings
and also simplifying

00:55:57.520 --> 00:56:01.440
the problem even further.

00:56:01.440 --> 00:56:03.300
Specifically, in
infinite horizon,

00:56:03.300 --> 00:56:05.120
Markov decision
process is a problem

00:56:05.120 --> 00:56:08.640
whereby as usual, we have a
state x on a computer science

00:56:08.640 --> 00:56:10.040
notation s.

00:56:10.040 --> 00:56:12.080
We have an action u.

00:56:12.080 --> 00:56:14.520
And we have a transition
function that tells us

00:56:14.520 --> 00:56:17.960
what the next state
xt will be given

00:56:17.960 --> 00:56:20.160
the current value of
the state xt minus 1

00:56:20.160 --> 00:56:22.460
and the current value of
the control ut minus 1.

00:56:25.600 --> 00:56:28.880
Now, since we're getting
closer to the RL formulation,

00:56:28.880 --> 00:56:30.740
typically in
reinforcement learning,

00:56:30.740 --> 00:56:32.210
people don't talk about cost.

00:56:32.210 --> 00:56:33.710
They talk about rewards.

00:56:33.710 --> 00:56:35.170
So you start minimizing a cost.

00:56:35.170 --> 00:56:37.510
We maximize a reward.

00:56:37.510 --> 00:56:39.610
That is just a
minor modification.

00:56:39.610 --> 00:56:42.190
Each problem can be
mapped in the other one

00:56:42.190 --> 00:56:45.210
by just warping minimization
with maximization

00:56:45.210 --> 00:56:49.150
and adding a sign minus
in front of the quantity

00:56:49.150 --> 00:56:50.830
you're interested in.

00:56:50.830 --> 00:56:53.270
We introduced the notion
of discount factor gamma.

00:56:53.270 --> 00:56:56.910
I will talk about it shortly.

00:56:56.910 --> 00:56:58.550
And so an
infinite-horizon problem

00:56:58.550 --> 00:57:03.510
is defined by these
five quantities here.

00:57:03.510 --> 00:57:04.955
We knew four of them.

00:57:04.955 --> 00:57:05.830
There is a fifth one.

00:57:05.830 --> 00:57:07.130
I'm introducing gamma.

00:57:07.130 --> 00:57:09.350
I'm going to explain
it in a second.

00:57:09.350 --> 00:57:13.710
The point I want to
make here is that now

00:57:13.710 --> 00:57:17.750
everything in this
formulation is stationary.

00:57:17.750 --> 00:57:20.470
What that means is that
the transition probability

00:57:20.470 --> 00:57:24.750
from the state at time t
minus 1 and control at time t

00:57:24.750 --> 00:57:28.110
minus 1 into the state
at time t does not

00:57:28.110 --> 00:57:33.490
depend on the specific
stage t minus 1.

00:57:33.490 --> 00:57:43.490
Remember that before, the
transition model was allowed

00:57:43.490 --> 00:57:45.270
to depend explicitly on time.

00:57:45.270 --> 00:57:47.310
So even if you had
the same state,

00:57:47.310 --> 00:57:50.290
same control, and you have the
same relation to the disturbance

00:57:50.290 --> 00:57:53.890
today and tomorrow, in general,
the transition to the next state

00:57:53.890 --> 00:57:56.210
might be different.

00:57:56.210 --> 00:57:58.168
In an infinite-horizon
MDP, we're

00:57:58.168 --> 00:58:00.210
making an assumption that
the problem formulation

00:58:00.210 --> 00:58:01.610
is stationary.

00:58:01.610 --> 00:58:04.210
Why do we make a
stationary assumption?

00:58:04.210 --> 00:58:06.690
One, again, because
oftentimes, it's

00:58:06.690 --> 00:58:09.530
a decent one for
processes whose dynamics

00:58:09.530 --> 00:58:11.930
don't change dramatically
from stage to stage.

00:58:11.930 --> 00:58:15.150
But two, in an infinite-horizon
MDP, as you can imagine,

00:58:15.150 --> 00:58:19.130
we are trying to use
some limit concept.

00:58:19.130 --> 00:58:20.230
And so doing limits.

00:58:20.230 --> 00:58:22.570
When the underlying problem
formulation changes,

00:58:22.570 --> 00:58:24.410
it will be very, very hard.

00:58:24.410 --> 00:58:30.150
So again, compromise between
being reasonable and making

00:58:30.150 --> 00:58:33.030
computation feasible.

00:58:33.030 --> 00:58:40.110
Now, our goal is to find
a policy that optimizes

00:58:40.110 --> 00:58:44.350
the expected cost-to-go.

00:58:44.350 --> 00:58:47.990
But now we are--
sorry, reward-to-go.

00:58:47.990 --> 00:58:51.670
So now we're talking about
rewards and known costs.

00:58:51.670 --> 00:58:53.630
And now we have
this additional term

00:58:53.630 --> 00:58:58.950
gamma that multiplies the
reward gamma to the power of t.

00:58:58.950 --> 00:59:01.630
So gamma is a number
between 0 and 1

00:59:01.630 --> 00:59:04.310
and is referred to as
the discount factor.

00:59:04.310 --> 00:59:09.190
So why do we multiply our
reward by this constant

00:59:09.190 --> 00:59:10.490
to the power of t?

00:59:10.490 --> 00:59:14.470
We didn't have that in the
finite-horizon formulation.

00:59:14.470 --> 00:59:17.730
Any hypothesis about
why people do that?

00:59:17.730 --> 00:59:20.230
You kind of started here
talking about rules--

00:59:20.230 --> 00:59:22.270
super far down the line is--

00:59:22.270 --> 00:59:22.910
Exactly.

00:59:22.910 --> 00:59:25.750
So that's the
intellectual explanation

00:59:25.750 --> 00:59:30.170
that in general, when you
try to optimize operations,

00:59:30.170 --> 00:59:32.210
you, of course, place
a lot of emphasis

00:59:32.210 --> 00:59:35.290
on the consequences of your
decisions in the next few time

00:59:35.290 --> 00:59:35.990
steps.

00:59:35.990 --> 00:59:39.450
You don't care too much about
what the consequences might

00:59:39.450 --> 00:59:43.030
be one month from now
or one year from now,

00:59:43.030 --> 00:59:45.010
which makes sense.

00:59:45.010 --> 00:59:49.470
But as usual, there is also
the pragmatic explanation.

00:59:49.470 --> 00:59:52.970
What is the pragmatic
explanation?

00:59:52.970 --> 00:59:54.290
They are here.

00:59:54.290 --> 00:59:55.330
Exactly.

00:59:55.330 --> 00:59:58.170
So since now we are in an
infinite-horizon setting,

00:59:58.170 --> 01:00:01.210
so this is a sum from
zero to plus infinity.

01:00:01.210 --> 01:00:04.490
And so in general, for
any reasonable reward,

01:00:04.490 --> 01:00:06.450
this will be equal
to plus infinity.

01:00:06.450 --> 01:00:10.030
So if all policies have a
cost equal to plus infinity,

01:00:10.030 --> 01:00:12.410
which one is optimal?

01:00:12.410 --> 01:00:13.110
Plus infinity.

01:00:13.110 --> 01:00:15.970
They are all phenomenal, right?

01:00:15.970 --> 01:00:20.090
So that's why you-- and you
put an exponential weighting

01:00:20.090 --> 01:00:25.680
because typically exponentials
kill a lot of functions.

01:00:25.680 --> 01:00:27.480
Is it a kind of
thing where people

01:00:27.480 --> 01:00:29.680
pre-append the quantities
of an exponential

01:00:29.680 --> 01:00:31.200
to ensure convergence.

01:00:31.200 --> 01:00:33.080
Anyway, just wanted
to highlight why

01:00:33.080 --> 01:00:34.740
people use the discount factor.

01:00:34.740 --> 01:00:35.920
Again, it makes sense.

01:00:35.920 --> 01:00:38.420
But also, practically--
there is a question?

01:00:38.420 --> 01:00:41.600
How do you get
the side straight?

01:00:41.600 --> 01:00:43.080
Do you have a question?

01:00:43.080 --> 01:00:44.520
Oh, no, I think we're good.

01:00:44.520 --> 01:00:45.880
OK, great.

01:00:45.880 --> 01:00:49.760
So infinite-horizon MDP,
problem is stationary.

01:00:49.760 --> 01:00:51.540
We're now focusing on rewards.

01:00:51.540 --> 01:00:54.920
We have this additional
discount factor in our modeling.

01:00:54.920 --> 01:00:57.640
Now, since the
problem is stationary

01:00:57.640 --> 01:01:00.000
and we are in an
infinite horizon,

01:01:00.000 --> 01:01:02.000
the policies, the
optimal policy,

01:01:02.000 --> 01:01:05.500
doesn't depend anymore
on the stage you are at.

01:01:05.500 --> 01:01:08.160
So you don't have a
P0, P1, P2 anymore.

01:01:08.160 --> 01:01:10.480
You just have pi.

01:01:10.480 --> 01:01:13.600
So we're just
optimizing over pi,

01:01:13.600 --> 01:01:17.360
a mapping between state
at any stage in time

01:01:17.360 --> 01:01:19.120
into a control action.

01:01:19.120 --> 01:01:20.880
Remember that before,
we were optimizing

01:01:20.880 --> 01:01:22.640
over pi 0, pi 1, pi 2.

01:01:22.640 --> 01:01:25.620
Here, we just optimize
over a single pi,

01:01:25.620 --> 01:01:27.180
because the
stationary policy that

01:01:27.180 --> 01:01:30.180
is a policy that does not
depend explicitly on time

01:01:30.180 --> 01:01:31.480
is indeed optimal.

01:01:31.480 --> 01:01:32.020
Yeah.

01:01:32.020 --> 01:01:34.140
Are you saying that
the optimal policy has

01:01:34.140 --> 01:01:36.820
to be stationary on this--

01:01:36.820 --> 01:01:39.360
We focus only on
stationary policies.

01:01:39.360 --> 01:01:42.900
Yes, that's what
we're saying here.

01:01:42.900 --> 01:01:43.420
Correct.

01:01:47.300 --> 01:01:47.920
OK.

01:01:47.920 --> 01:01:54.560
So then the question is, how
do we solve such problems?

01:01:57.860 --> 01:01:58.460
Guesses?

01:02:05.380 --> 01:02:08.220
Well, we try to reuse
whatever we learned

01:02:08.220 --> 01:02:11.740
from a finite-horizon
optimal control, which

01:02:11.740 --> 01:02:17.380
is the notion of a
Bellman equation.

01:02:17.380 --> 01:02:27.000
So trying to exploit
that intuition.

01:02:27.000 --> 01:02:29.460
Remember that when we were in
the finite-horizon setting,

01:02:29.460 --> 01:02:33.800
we said that the optimal
cost or optimal rewards

01:02:33.800 --> 01:02:37.240
satisfy a recursive equation.

01:02:37.240 --> 01:02:42.440
So it's natural to wonder if
an infinite-horizon setting--

01:02:42.440 --> 01:02:45.560
if you let the number of stages
to go to infinity-- basically,

01:02:45.560 --> 01:02:47.880
you take a limit on both sides--

01:02:47.880 --> 01:02:51.800
that stuff will
converge to something.

01:02:51.800 --> 01:02:53.580
So basically, remember
that you have--

01:03:04.680 --> 01:03:11.840
that J of k xk is equal
to maximum over uk

01:03:11.840 --> 01:03:16.340
of expectation over wk of--

01:03:18.920 --> 01:03:20.400
actually, let me
do it with a cost

01:03:20.400 --> 01:03:23.120
since we were talking about
costs with the J formulation.

01:03:26.540 --> 01:03:27.180
Oops.

01:03:27.180 --> 01:03:39.660
There is a Jk, xk, uk, wk
plus Jk plus 1, xk plus 1.

01:03:39.660 --> 01:03:50.500
So assuming our
problem is stationary,

01:03:50.500 --> 01:03:53.800
does this stuff
converge to something?

01:03:58.620 --> 01:04:01.020
And if this stuff
converge to something

01:04:01.020 --> 01:04:04.140
and I take the
limit here and here,

01:04:04.140 --> 01:04:07.440
do I get a sort of
stationary Bellman equation?

01:04:10.540 --> 01:04:11.620
The answer is yes.

01:04:11.620 --> 01:04:16.220
So actually, the cost function
in the limited horizons

01:04:16.220 --> 01:04:19.810
of infinity does converge
to a steady-state quantity.

01:04:19.810 --> 01:04:23.330
And that steady-state
quantity satisfies not

01:04:23.330 --> 01:04:25.370
recursive equations
such as there

01:04:25.370 --> 01:04:29.490
by fixed-point equation,
which is represented here.

01:04:29.490 --> 01:04:32.850
So this is the extension
of the Bellman equation

01:04:32.850 --> 01:04:39.050
to an infinite-horizon
setting, whereby V,

01:04:39.050 --> 01:04:40.910
which is the same as J.

01:04:40.910 --> 01:04:43.410
But when people
talk about rewards,

01:04:43.410 --> 01:04:48.210
they like to refer to it
as V. V stands for value.

01:04:48.210 --> 01:04:51.690
So reward, values, cost, J's.

01:04:51.690 --> 01:04:54.290
I don't know what J
stands for actually.

01:04:54.290 --> 01:04:57.490
Anyway, so V star x
is equal to maximum

01:04:57.490 --> 01:05:01.090
over u of the immediate
reward plus the expectation

01:05:01.090 --> 01:05:02.310
of the reward-to-go.

01:05:06.330 --> 01:05:08.970
So in the
infinite-horizon problem,

01:05:08.970 --> 01:05:12.290
one can prove that the
optimal value function--

01:05:12.290 --> 01:05:16.770
so the value of the problem
assuming that you start

01:05:16.770 --> 01:05:20.310
at x and you follow an
optimal policy-- satisfies

01:05:20.310 --> 01:05:24.190
a fixed-point equation, whereby
V star x is equal to max over

01:05:24.190 --> 01:05:30.910
u of R plus expected cost-to-go
discounted by gamma, one step

01:05:30.910 --> 01:05:32.430
discounting factor.

01:05:32.430 --> 01:05:34.990
So we take this as a fact.

01:05:34.990 --> 01:05:40.350
There is a bit of a proof that
uses some limit considerations.

01:05:40.350 --> 01:05:42.950
But again, from
intuition standpoint,

01:05:42.950 --> 01:05:45.010
I'm trying to give you
some justification.

01:05:45.010 --> 01:05:46.990
The justification
is that this looks

01:05:46.990 --> 01:05:49.670
very similar to the
Bellman equation.

01:05:49.670 --> 01:05:51.670
But in an
infinite-horizon setting,

01:05:51.670 --> 01:05:55.190
the recursive equation
becomes just an equation.

01:05:58.382 --> 01:05:59.230
Yeah.

01:05:59.230 --> 01:06:04.030
When you're solving these, how
do you inform the algorithm

01:06:04.030 --> 01:06:05.570
to not keep searching?

01:06:05.570 --> 01:06:07.870
Because obviously, with
the discount factor,

01:06:07.870 --> 01:06:10.950
you can probably see at a
point like clearly some earlier

01:06:10.950 --> 01:06:13.530
stopping point would be more
optimal than a later one.

01:06:13.530 --> 01:06:16.322
But how do you
distinguish when it's not

01:06:16.322 --> 01:06:17.530
even worth to keep searching?

01:06:17.530 --> 01:06:21.330
Because maybe there is some
minimum like if that's what--

01:06:21.330 --> 01:06:21.830
Yeah.

01:06:21.830 --> 01:06:25.010
So keep in mind that
here, I'm not giving you

01:06:25.010 --> 01:06:27.410
an actual algorithm yet.

01:06:27.410 --> 01:06:29.930
I'm just highlighting
a key property

01:06:29.930 --> 01:06:33.850
of infinite-horizon MDPs,
whereby the optimal value

01:06:33.850 --> 01:06:37.850
functions satisfy an equation.

01:06:37.850 --> 01:06:40.130
How we translate this
information into a search

01:06:40.130 --> 01:06:42.090
algorithm, that's
something we'll

01:06:42.090 --> 01:06:44.370
see at the beginning
of the next lecture.

01:06:44.370 --> 01:06:48.650
And basically, we can do in a
couple of ways, value iteration

01:06:48.650 --> 01:06:51.010
algorithms and policy
iteration algorithms.

01:06:51.010 --> 01:06:54.610
For now, this is just
a derived property

01:06:54.610 --> 01:06:56.930
of our problem formulation.

01:06:56.930 --> 01:07:01.050
Just so happens
that if you are not

01:07:01.050 --> 01:07:03.190
interested in the
optimal value function,

01:07:03.190 --> 01:07:06.490
but you're actually interested
in the following question,

01:07:06.490 --> 01:07:10.330
if I give you any
policy pi, what

01:07:10.330 --> 01:07:16.230
is the cumulative reward that
that policy is going to collect?

01:07:16.230 --> 01:07:21.070
Turns out that the
value for that policy

01:07:21.070 --> 01:07:25.430
satisfies a very similar
fixed-point equation.

01:07:25.430 --> 01:07:27.550
But now, we don't have
a maximization over u

01:07:27.550 --> 01:07:30.310
anymore because we're
fixing the policy.

01:07:30.310 --> 01:07:32.910
And then the computation
of the u's are simply

01:07:32.910 --> 01:07:35.090
with respect to the
policy pi that is given.

01:07:42.910 --> 01:07:45.290
Note that this is given to you.

01:07:45.290 --> 01:07:47.190
This is given to you.

01:07:47.190 --> 01:07:53.070
So assuming that x has a
finite number of realizations,

01:07:53.070 --> 01:07:55.290
this is actually a linear
system of equations

01:07:55.290 --> 01:07:57.230
whereas with the
value function V.

01:07:57.230 --> 01:08:01.550
So you could potentially
solve this equation by solving

01:08:01.550 --> 01:08:03.210
a linear system of equations.

01:08:06.510 --> 01:08:10.910
Can you repeat why previously
for dynamic programming

01:08:10.910 --> 01:08:15.740
the cost function depended
on the service like here?

01:08:15.740 --> 01:08:19.060
Yes, here, depends still
on the disturbance.

01:08:19.060 --> 01:08:24.140
But the stochastic dependency
is more cryptically captured

01:08:24.140 --> 01:08:25.800
by this transition function.

01:08:25.800 --> 01:08:31.819
That is, T is the probability
of going into a new state x

01:08:31.819 --> 01:08:35.180
prime given x and u.

01:08:35.180 --> 01:08:37.670
So is what I was saying before.

01:08:37.670 --> 01:08:39.920
And here, I made a little
bit of a change of notation,

01:08:39.920 --> 01:08:42.340
as I mentioned from the
control theory notation

01:08:42.340 --> 01:08:45.060
to the computer
science notation.

01:08:45.060 --> 01:08:55.380
So you can write xk plus
1 equal f of xk, uk, wk,

01:08:55.380 --> 01:09:01.340
and then say the wk follows some
probability Pk of conditional xk

01:09:01.340 --> 01:09:02.340
uk.

01:09:02.340 --> 01:09:05.460
Or equivalently,
you can say that--

01:09:05.460 --> 01:09:11.220
so what I give you is a P and f
in order to compute the next x.

01:09:11.220 --> 01:09:15.560
Or I can just give you
this object T of xk

01:09:15.560 --> 01:09:19.479
plus 1 given xk uk.

01:09:19.479 --> 01:09:21.680
So this is directly the
probability distribution

01:09:21.680 --> 01:09:24.600
over next states given x and u.

01:09:24.600 --> 01:09:26.979
The two are actually identical.

01:09:26.979 --> 01:09:28.920
They can be mapped
to each other.

01:09:28.920 --> 01:09:30.500
The difference is
that notationally,

01:09:30.500 --> 01:09:32.979
the dependence on
disturbance here is explicit.

01:09:32.979 --> 01:09:35.160
Here is a bit more cryptic.

01:09:35.160 --> 01:09:39.720
But the point being, the
dynamics are still stochastic,

01:09:39.720 --> 01:09:42.120
and the stochasticity is
captured by the probability

01:09:42.120 --> 01:09:44.640
distribution over next
states given x and u.

01:09:44.640 --> 01:09:47.000
Note that this model
is clearly Markovian,

01:09:47.000 --> 01:09:49.200
in the sense that the
dependence in the past

01:09:49.200 --> 01:09:52.000
is only with respect to the
current value of the state

01:09:52.000 --> 01:09:53.500
x and the current action u.

01:09:57.880 --> 01:09:58.540
All right.

01:09:58.540 --> 01:10:00.220
One more thing and
then we wrap it up.

01:10:00.220 --> 01:10:02.400
I'll talk about
algorithms to solve

01:10:02.400 --> 01:10:05.220
for the infinite-horizon
Bellman equation.

01:10:05.220 --> 01:10:07.280
And next time, it
won't be too long.

01:10:07.280 --> 01:10:10.160
I'll need only 15, 20 minutes.

01:10:10.160 --> 01:10:12.380
There is an alternative
way of writing

01:10:12.380 --> 01:10:18.420
the infinite-horizon Bellman
equation that seems very benign,

01:10:18.420 --> 01:10:20.860
but actually, it's been
quite revolutionary,

01:10:20.860 --> 01:10:25.460
especially to derive
learning-based algorithms.

01:10:25.460 --> 01:10:27.940
Specifically, let's
define something that

01:10:27.940 --> 01:10:32.300
is referred to as a Q function.

01:10:32.300 --> 01:10:35.500
The Q function is simply
the right-hand side

01:10:35.500 --> 01:10:38.060
of the infinite-horizon
Bellman equation.

01:10:38.060 --> 01:10:40.660
And it has the following
interpretation.

01:10:40.660 --> 01:10:43.860
Is the cumulative
reward that you

01:10:43.860 --> 01:10:51.660
collect assuming that you
start at x, you play control u.

01:10:51.660 --> 01:10:56.180
And then you act
optimally after them

01:10:56.180 --> 01:10:58.000
according to the policy pi star.

01:11:01.060 --> 01:11:04.700
So if you are a state
x and you use control u

01:11:04.700 --> 01:11:09.040
and then you optimally
with from the next stage

01:11:09.040 --> 01:11:11.930
onward your cumulative
cost is R x,

01:11:11.930 --> 01:11:16.160
u plus the optimal reward-to-go
from the perspective

01:11:16.160 --> 01:11:17.105
of the next stage.

01:11:17.105 --> 01:11:18.980
But, of course, the next
state is stochastic,

01:11:18.980 --> 01:11:21.920
so you have to average
the different realizations

01:11:21.920 --> 01:11:25.960
of the next state.

01:11:25.960 --> 01:11:29.200
So you say, who cares?

01:11:29.200 --> 01:11:32.040
I'll tell you in one
second why you care.

01:11:32.040 --> 01:11:36.240
But first, I want to
show that assuming

01:11:36.240 --> 01:11:41.320
that you care about the
Q star, Q functions,

01:11:41.320 --> 01:11:45.120
the Q function satisfies a
fundamental equation that

01:11:45.120 --> 01:11:49.040
is essentially the same
as the Bellman equation,

01:11:49.040 --> 01:11:51.720
but for Q functions
specifically.

01:11:51.720 --> 01:11:59.720
And this seems to be like
a little bit circuitous.

01:11:59.720 --> 01:12:04.386
So we said that the Q
star is by definition R x,

01:12:04.386 --> 01:12:09.950
u plus gamma sum
over the next state

01:12:09.950 --> 01:12:14.990
x prime of the realization of
the next states times V star

01:12:14.990 --> 01:12:17.350
x prime.

01:12:17.350 --> 01:12:21.170
So that's the
definition of a cube.

01:12:26.750 --> 01:12:31.310
But then we also have
the condition that V star

01:12:31.310 --> 01:12:36.870
x again following our
definition is equal to max over

01:12:36.870 --> 01:12:38.590
u of Q star x, u.

01:12:41.350 --> 01:12:43.530
So you plug this into here.

01:12:43.530 --> 01:12:48.030
And now you have a fixed-point
equation for the Q functions.

01:12:48.030 --> 01:12:54.070
OK, so that's fine.

01:12:54.070 --> 01:12:57.670
But then the question for
me should be, why do we

01:12:57.670 --> 01:12:58.970
care about Q functions?

01:13:02.870 --> 01:13:04.950
In principle, you
don't care about them.

01:13:04.950 --> 01:13:07.050
But let's assume--
and, of course, we

01:13:07.050 --> 01:13:11.810
will revisit this setting
at a great extent.

01:13:11.810 --> 01:13:22.090
Let's assume that now some part
of the model are not known.

01:13:22.090 --> 01:13:31.170
So you don't know this
probability distribution T.

01:13:31.170 --> 01:13:33.797
And you have some algorithm,
learning algorithm--

01:13:33.797 --> 01:13:36.130
reinforcement learning algorithm
or imitation learning--

01:13:36.130 --> 01:13:37.880
let's say reinforcement
learning algorithm

01:13:37.880 --> 01:13:42.130
that allows you to learn
approximately the Q functions.

01:13:42.130 --> 01:13:45.130
We'll see which
algorithms we can use.

01:13:45.130 --> 01:13:47.050
So some part of the
MDP problems are not

01:13:47.050 --> 01:13:50.810
known anymore, like the
transition kernel is not known.

01:13:50.810 --> 01:13:52.770
But we're learning an
approximation for Q.

01:13:52.770 --> 01:13:56.170
And now I'm asking you to
compute the optimal action given

01:13:56.170 --> 01:13:59.010
that you are at state x.

01:13:59.010 --> 01:13:59.950
So what do you do?

01:13:59.950 --> 01:14:03.510
Well, if an oracle
gives you the Q star x,

01:14:03.510 --> 01:14:10.470
u, then your optimal
action is simply the one

01:14:10.470 --> 01:14:15.510
that maximizes the Q. OK.

01:14:15.510 --> 01:14:20.190
So basically, it's an oracle
gives you the optimal Q

01:14:20.190 --> 01:14:21.070
function.

01:14:21.070 --> 01:14:23.215
Then computing an optimal
action is trivial.

01:14:23.215 --> 01:14:24.840
You just solve the
optimization-- well,

01:14:24.840 --> 01:14:26.930
T. I mean, it could be a
high-dimensional problem,

01:14:26.930 --> 01:14:29.390
but at least you
have a way to do so.

01:14:29.390 --> 01:14:34.310
Say that actually oracle
gives you the V star function,

01:14:34.310 --> 01:14:36.430
the optimal value function.

01:14:36.430 --> 01:14:38.330
How do you compute
the optimal action?

01:14:41.070 --> 01:14:48.336
Well, now the optimal action
is going to be max of u of R

01:14:48.336 --> 01:14:57.530
x, u plus gamma sum of T x
prime x, u V star x prime.

01:15:01.790 --> 01:15:05.850
And so even if we give you
the optimal value function

01:15:05.850 --> 01:15:08.370
to compute the
optimal action, you

01:15:08.370 --> 01:15:13.152
need to know the
transition kernel.

01:15:13.152 --> 01:15:14.610
But if you're in
a learning setting

01:15:14.610 --> 01:15:17.068
where we assume that we don't
have access to the transition

01:15:17.068 --> 01:15:19.170
kernel, that becomes a problem.

01:15:19.170 --> 01:15:23.250
That's why this somewhat
very benign reformulation

01:15:23.250 --> 01:15:26.450
of the problem has been
extremely influential in order

01:15:26.450 --> 01:15:28.810
to derive learning algorithms,
because that allows

01:15:28.810 --> 01:15:33.130
you to much more directly
find actional algorithms

01:15:33.130 --> 01:15:37.897
for optimal action estimation.

01:15:37.897 --> 01:15:40.230
Just to want to-- the first
sentence on Q function said,

01:15:40.230 --> 01:15:41.990
OK, this is completely useless.

01:15:41.990 --> 01:15:45.910
I'm just writing one thing
in terms of another quantity.

01:15:45.910 --> 01:15:46.410
Why?

01:15:46.410 --> 01:15:48.690
So people typically don't
really discuss that.

01:15:48.690 --> 01:15:51.330
But that's a big
difference, that if you then

01:15:51.330 --> 01:15:54.190
reframe the problem through
the notion of Q function,

01:15:54.190 --> 01:15:57.730
then backtracking and
optimal control action

01:15:57.730 --> 01:15:59.410
becomes much more
natural in the setting

01:15:59.410 --> 01:16:02.620
where some elements of the
MDP problem is not known.

01:16:02.620 --> 01:16:04.740
For example, the
transition probabilities.

01:16:08.000 --> 01:16:09.280
Make sense?

01:16:09.280 --> 01:16:13.640
So what I'm going to see next
time is a couple of algorithms

01:16:13.640 --> 01:16:14.580
to--

01:16:14.580 --> 01:16:20.320
oh, given a policy, there
is also an analog equation

01:16:20.320 --> 01:16:23.660
to compute a Q function
for the specific policy.

01:16:23.660 --> 01:16:27.960
So Q pi x, u is the value
of the problem assuming

01:16:27.960 --> 01:16:29.280
that you are at state x.

01:16:29.280 --> 01:16:34.280
You use control u, and then you
use the policy pi for all stages

01:16:34.280 --> 01:16:35.240
afterwards.

01:16:35.240 --> 01:16:36.840
So what we're going
to see next time

01:16:36.840 --> 01:16:40.027
is a couple of algorithms to
solve the infinite-horizon MDP

01:16:40.027 --> 01:16:42.360
problem, value iteration
algorithm, and policy iteration

01:16:42.360 --> 01:16:43.648
algorithms.

01:16:43.648 --> 01:16:45.440
And then we're going
to move into the topic

01:16:45.440 --> 01:16:48.960
of continuous-time,
closed-loop optimal control.

01:16:48.960 --> 01:16:51.360
We'll get back to
infinite-horizon MDPs

01:16:51.360 --> 01:16:53.080
and their extension
to the setting

01:16:53.080 --> 01:16:56.150
of learning-based control
in a couple of weeks.

